#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/getting_started/tutorial-5-run-experiments.ipynb:539
msgid ""
"The available methods are ``current``, ``c_rate``, ``voltage``, ``power``, "
"and ``resistance``. These methods also take optional keyword arguments, such "
"as the period, temperature, tags or starting times (a complete list can be "
"found in `the documentation <https://docs.pybamm.org/en/stable/source/api/"
"experiment/experiment_steps.html>`__)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/getting_started/tutorial-5-run-experiments.ipynb:541
msgid ""
"These methods can also be used for drive cycles. In this case, the ``value`` "
"argument should be a 2-column array, where the first column is time in "
"seconds (should start at zero) and the second column the values (i.e. "
"current, voltage, power...). Here is an example for a synthetically defined "
"drive cycle:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/getting_started/tutorial-5-run-experiments.ipynb:-1
msgid "[10]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/getting_started/tutorial-5-run-experiments.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"t = np.linspace(0, 1, 60)\n"
"sin_t = 0.5 * np.sin(2 * np.pi * t)\n"
"drive_cycle_power = np.column_stack([t, sin_t])\n"
"experiment = pybamm.Experiment([pybamm.step.power(drive_cycle_power)])\n"
"sim = pybamm.Simulation(model, experiment=experiment)\n"
"sim.solve()\n"
"sim.plot()"
msgstr ""

